WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.400
If you’re anything like me,

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:06.560
you have lots of ambitious
plans for the future.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:09.160
I’ve got a whole series of
new books I want to write,

00:00:09.160 --> 00:00:11.400
thousands more
videos to script.

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:13.360
But guess what?

00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:16.760
None of us may be able to do
any of the things we want to do

00:00:16.760 --> 00:00:20.240
if we don't have our health.

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:22.480
Welcome to the
NutritionFacts Podcast.

00:00:22.480 --> 00:00:25.440
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:30.240
Today we hear about new research
on Alzehimer's from Dr. Dean Ornish.

00:00:30.240 --> 00:00:33.240
And we share the results of the
first randomized controlled trial

00:00:33.240 --> 00:00:37.040
investigating whether a plant-
based diet and lifestyle program

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.200
may reverse the course of
early-stage Alzheimer’s disease.

00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:44.960
Dr. Dean Ornish was
the first to show,

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.320
in a randomized
controlled trial,

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:50.000
that a plant-based diet
and lifestyle program

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:52.960
could apparently reverse
the progression

00:00:52.960 --> 00:00:56.040
of our number one killer,
heart disease.

00:00:56.040 --> 00:00:59.440
Opening up arteries without
drugs, without surgery.

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.600
Then, he showed the same
plant-based program

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:04.240
could potentially
reverse the course

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:06.800
of early-stage
prostate cancer

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:08.960
and also elongate
telomeres,

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:12.640
suggesting an anti-
aging effect as well.

00:01:12.640 --> 00:01:15.240
But when he told me
he was going to see

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.304
if he could reverse the progression
of Alzheimer's disease,

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:23.000
Surely, he was biting off a little
more than he could chew.

00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:26.760
Dementia is the most
feared condition of later life.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:29.240
There’s a common
misconception

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:30.880
that we have no control

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.240
over whether we
develop dementia,

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.600
but the good news is

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.160
that although Alzheimer’s
may be incurable,

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:42.280
at least it is preventable.

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:44.360
There is an emerging consensus

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:49.280
that “what’s good for our hearts
is also good for our heads,”

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.160
because clogging of the
arteries inside the brain

00:01:53.160 --> 00:01:56.880
with atherosclerotic plaque
is thought to play a role

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.438
in the development of
Alzheimer’s dementia.

00:01:59.760 --> 00:02:01.600
Too much cholesterol
in our blood

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:05.440
is unanimously recognized
to be a risk factor

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:08.800
for the development
of Alzheimer’s disease.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:12.120
Those with a total cholesterol
of 225 mg/dL or more

00:02:12.120 --> 00:02:15.280
may have nearly
25 times the odds

00:02:15.280 --> 00:02:17.800
of ending up with amyloid
plaques in their brain

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.400
10 to 15 years later.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:24.080
After all, what is the
Alzheimer’s gene, APOE?

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:28.400
It codes for the major cholesterol
carrier inside the brain.

00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.240
This may explain the
so-called Nigerian paradox:

00:02:32.240 --> 00:02:36.040
They have among the highest
rates of the Alzheimer’s gene

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:39.520
but some of the lowest
rates of Alzheimer’s disease.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:41.080
How is that possible?

00:02:41.080 --> 00:02:45.240
Genes load the gun,
but lifestyle pulls the trigger.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.080
The paradox may be explained
by their low cholesterol levels,

00:02:49.080 --> 00:02:52.640
probably due to their
diets low in animal fat.

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:57.520
So, in terms of dietary guidelines
for the prevention of Alzheimer’s,

00:02:57.520 --> 00:02:59.840
we should center our
diets around vegetables,

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:05.720
legumes, fruits,
and whole grains.

00:03:05.720 --> 00:03:09.680
In other words, the dietary
pillar of lifestyle medicine:

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.480
whole food,
plant-based nutrition.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:19.280
Or, if that’s too complicated,
"plants, plants, and more plants."

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:21.880
That may help explain
why vegetarians

00:03:21.880 --> 00:03:27.160
may be up to three times less likely
to become demented later in life.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.160
But it’s not all-or-nothing.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:35.000
Even just substituting 5%
of animal protein with plant protein

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:40.520
appears to significantly reduce
the risk of dying from dementia.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:43.680
But prevention isn’t sexy.

00:03:43.680 --> 00:03:47.840
When prevention works,
nothing happens,

00:03:47.840 --> 00:03:51.560
but the same diet and lifestyle
that helps prevent heart disease

00:03:51.560 --> 00:03:54.160
was proven to
help reverse it.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:57.200
Until then, it was believed
that heart disease progression

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:00.920
could only be slowed,
not stopped or reversed,

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:05.720
similar to how Alzheimer’s
disease is viewed today.

00:04:05.720 --> 00:04:08.520
So, what if you put
people with Alzheimer’s

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:10.680
on the same plant-
based program?

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:16.200
You don’t know until
you put it to the test.

00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:17.760
A randomized,
controlled,

00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.680
phase 2 clinical trial

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:22.320
to see if the progression
of Alzheimer’s disease

00:04:22.320 --> 00:04:26.280
may be slowed, stopped,
or perhaps even reversed.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.960
They randomized about
50 men and women

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:31.880
diagnosed with early-
stage Alzheimer’s

00:04:31.880 --> 00:04:35.440
to either make no lifestyle
changes for 20 weeks

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.520
or eat a whole food,
plant-based diet

00:04:39.520 --> 00:04:42.080
(with supplements
like vitamin B12),

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:43.840
moderate exercise

00:04:43.840 --> 00:04:46.280
(like walking half
an hour a day),

00:04:46.280 --> 00:04:48.160
stress management

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:50.920
(like relaxing with
breathing exercises),

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:55.280
and getting group
support (over Zoom).

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.040
They measured
standard tests

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:00.440
of cognition and function
before and after in each group,

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:03.040
as well as objective
experimental biomarkers

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:04.960
of disease progression.

00:05:04.960 --> 00:05:07.960
On the Clinical Dementia
Rating Global scale,

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.800
which is used to stage
the severity of dementia,

00:05:11.800 --> 00:05:15.920
the control group
continued to get worse,

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.640
but the diet and lifestyle
group started to get better.

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:25.000
People diagnosed with
Alzheimer’s getting better?

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:27.280
The same seemed to
happen when measured

00:05:27.280 --> 00:05:30.440
with the Alzheimer’s Disease
Assessment Scale,

00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:34.320
though this did not reach
statistical significance.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:35.400
Using what’s called the

00:05:35.400 --> 00:05:38.400
Clinical Dementia Rating
Sum of Boxes scoring,

00:05:38.400 --> 00:05:41.280
both groups
continued to deteriorate,

00:05:41.280 --> 00:05:46.320
but the decline was significantly
less in the healthy living group.

00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:48.280
Overall, using
what’s called the

00:05:48.280 --> 00:05:51.720
Clinical Global Impression
of Change scoring,

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:55.760
most of the people in the
control group kept getting worse

00:05:55.760 --> 00:06:01.600
and none showed any improvement,
which is what you'd expect with Alzheimer's,

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:06.280
whereas about 40% of those
in the diet and lifestyle group

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.760
appeared to be getting
better within five months

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.920
of eating and
living healthier.

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:15.280
Why did some get
better and others not?

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.200
Well, the more they complied
with the recommendations,

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:22.600
the greater the beneficial impact
on their cognition and function.

00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.160
This helps to
explain why studies

00:06:24.160 --> 00:06:26.800
of less-intensive
lifestyle interventions

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.440
were not sufficient to
stop disease progression,

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:34.160
let alone actually improve
cognition and function.

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:37.880
The biggest limitation
of the study is that,

00:06:37.880 --> 00:06:40.600
unlike drug trials where
you can give people

00:06:40.600 --> 00:06:44.040
a disguised placebo
sugar pill,

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:46.880
when a study involves major
diet and lifestyle changes,

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:50.200
you can’t rule out
the placebo effect,

00:06:50.200 --> 00:06:53.240
especially for self-
reported, subjective

00:06:53.240 --> 00:06:56.480
“How’s your memory been?”
type-questions.

00:06:56.480 --> 00:06:58.600
But the researchers
also measured

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:02.800
objective investigational biomarkers
of disease progression

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.000
and saw the same
trajectory—

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.360
improvements in the
interventional group

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:10.320
and worsening in
the control group,

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:13.600
with the same apparent
dose-response effect,

00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:16.000
meaning the more they improved
their diet and lifestyle,

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:19.160
the more dramatic the effect.

00:07:19.160 --> 00:07:22.680
Compare that to the
latest Alzheimer’s drugs,

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.680
which may not
even work at all.

00:07:25.680 --> 00:07:29.520
All you may get
for your $56,000

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:36.200
is a one-in-three chance of
swelling or bleeding in your brain.

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:38.480
When the U.S. Food
and Drug Administration

00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:40.560
approved the
drug anyway,

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.720
the head of the American
Geriatrics Society replied,

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:48.160
“My head just exploded.”

00:07:48.160 --> 00:07:51.920
The bottom line is
there is only one diet

00:07:51.920 --> 00:07:55.680
that's ever been shown to help
reverse our leading cause of death,

00:07:55.680 --> 00:07:58.120
heart disease,
in the majority of patients:

00:07:58.120 --> 00:07:59.440
a plant-based diet.

00:08:00.040 --> 00:08:02.720
If that’s all a plant-
based diet could do—

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:06.520
reverse the number one
killer of men and women,

00:08:06.520 --> 00:08:10.280
then shouldn’t that be the default
diet until proven otherwise?

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:12.080
And the fact that it
can also be so effective

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:14.200
in preventing, arresting,
and reversing

00:08:14.200 --> 00:08:16.400
the progression of
other leading killers,

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:19.120
like high blood pressure,
type 2 diabetes,

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:22.080
and now maybe even early-
stage Alzheimer’s disease

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.360
would seem to make the
case for plant-based eating

00:08:24.360 --> 00:08:27.360
simply overwhelming.

00:08:27.360 --> 00:08:28.240
In our next story,

00:08:28.240 --> 00:08:33.960
we look at the human impact of improving the
cognition and function of Alzheimer’s patients.

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:35.200
I released a video

00:08:35.200 --> 00:08:38.000
profiling Dr. Dean Ornish’s
landmark new study

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:39.360
in the leading peer-reviewed

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.040
Alzheimer’s translational
research journal,

00:08:42.040 --> 00:08:43.680
a randomized controlled trial

00:08:43.680 --> 00:08:46.360
showing that a plant-based
diet and lifestyle program

00:08:46.360 --> 00:08:49.480
may significantly improve
cognition and function

00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:55.160
after 20 weeks in many patients
with early-stage Alzheimer's disease.

00:08:55.160 --> 00:08:58.640
About 70% remained stable
or actually improved

00:08:58.640 --> 00:09:00.680
in the plant-based
intervention group,

00:09:00.680 --> 00:09:01.960
whereas,
in the control group,

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:08.120
about 70% got worse and
not a single person got better.

00:09:08.120 --> 00:09:11.080
But no matter how
significant the results,

00:09:11.080 --> 00:09:15.560
the dry statistics just don’t
convey the human impact.

00:09:15.560 --> 00:09:17.520
Many patients who
experienced improvement

00:09:17.520 --> 00:09:20.720
reported regaining cognition
and function they had lost,

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:25.040
but how does that actually
translate into real life?

00:09:25.040 --> 00:09:28.520
Well, for example, several
patients in the intervention group

00:09:28.520 --> 00:09:32.880
reported that they had been unable
to read a book or watch a movie

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:36.120
because they kept forgetting what
they just read or watched

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:38.280
and had to keep starting over,

00:09:38.280 --> 00:09:40.960
but after the plant-based diet
and lifestyle changes,

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:42.520
they got better,

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:46.080
such that now they were able
to read and watch shows again.

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:48.960
One individual reported that
it used to take him weeks

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:52.120
to ﬁnish reading a book,
but after participating in the study,

00:09:52.120 --> 00:09:55.080
he was able to do it
in a matter of days.

00:09:55.080 --> 00:09:58.160
Another participant,
a former business executive,

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:02.440
reported regaining the ability to manage
his own ﬁnances and investments.

00:10:02.440 --> 00:10:05.520
“It was so much
a part of my life”, he said,

00:10:05.520 --> 00:10:07.440
“who I am, and who I was—

00:10:07.440 --> 00:10:11.400
it was hard saying that
part of me was just gone.

00:10:11.400 --> 00:10:15.440
But now I’m back to
reconciling our ﬁnances monthly;

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:17.920
I keep up to date
on our investments.

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:21.440
A lot of self-worth
comes back.”

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:24.880
Another one said that for ﬁve
years she had been unable

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:28.240
to prepare their family
business’s ﬁnancial reports,

00:10:28.240 --> 00:10:30.560
but now she is able
to do so accurately.

00:10:30.560 --> 00:10:34.080
“A deep sense of
identity is returning.

00:10:34.080 --> 00:10:35.760
It’s given me a
new lease on life,

00:10:35.760 --> 00:10:40.600
and yet it’s a familiarity and something
I’ve always prided myself on.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:45.880
I’m coming back like I was prior to the
Alzheimer’s disease being diagnosed.

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:52.800
I feel like I’m me again—
an older but better version of me.”

00:10:52.800 --> 00:10:58.680
But even words are hard to
describe such transformations.

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:03.360
One of Dr. Ornish’s study participants
gave us permission to share his story.

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:08.440
Dan Jones, a musician at
military events and parades,

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:12.280
who was diagnosed
with Alzheimer's disease.

00:11:12.280 --> 00:11:14.920
"What do you notice that you could
do now that you couldn't do before?"

00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:16.280
"Well, for one thing,

00:11:16.280 --> 00:11:22.960
I had a set of about eight tunes
that I played in the parade.

00:11:23.800 --> 00:11:28.680
And I could just go straight through
them from one into the other.

00:11:29.640 --> 00:11:32.200
I played for an hour and
a half without stopping.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:34.440
I think it was pretty close.

00:11:34.440 --> 00:11:37.840
They move darn
slow in that parade.

00:11:37.840 --> 00:11:39.520
I was leaving them
behind walking.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:42.160
I kept having to stop and
wait for them to catch up."

00:11:42.160 --> 00:11:44.340
"Was that something different?"

00:11:44.340 --> 00:11:45.000
"Yeah."

00:11:45.000 --> 00:11:46.280
"And what was?"

00:11:46.280 --> 00:11:48.800
"Well, that I could
remember the order.

00:11:48.800 --> 00:11:53.080
I could make the transition from
tune to tune without messing up

00:11:53.080 --> 00:11:56.160
and getting confused about
which tune I was playing.

00:11:56.160 --> 00:11:59.240
Those were all problems I'd
had when we were playing

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:02.660
at the graduation ceremony.

00:12:02.660 --> 00:12:03.200
You know,

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:05.720
I'd forget which tune
was coming next.

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:08.920
I'd get worried about it,
and I'd start making mistakes.

00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.120
And this just went
like clockwork.

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:15.200
A lot of it had to do with practice,
but the tunes we played before,

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:18.040
I had played hundreds of times.

00:12:18.040 --> 00:12:22.200
And I was messing up,

00:12:22.200 --> 00:12:26.440
and in the parade,
I hardly messed up at all."

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:29.520
"And you were messing up because
you had a hard time remembering?"

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:32.000
"Because I had a hard
time remembering.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:34.160
I'd get confused
about different tunes.

00:12:34.160 --> 00:12:36.560
It was really, yeah, it was
not a pleasant experience.

00:12:36.560 --> 00:12:39.360
It was unpleasant enough that,
like I said,

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:40.860
I went home and put them down.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:44.520
And now, there was one
day a couple of weeks ago

00:12:44.520 --> 00:12:47.840
when I really had
the drones in tune.

00:12:47.840 --> 00:12:48.800
It was in the evening,

00:12:48.800 --> 00:12:50.420
I was out on the
front porch playing,

00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:51.960
and it was just so beautiful.

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:55.316
I played for 2 hours.
I just stood there and played."

00:12:55.316 --> 00:12:57.840
I'm crying because I'm happy.

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:03.080
It was just so nice to
be able to do that again."

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.360
"Did that make you feel
better about yourself too?"

00:13:05.360 --> 00:13:06.240
"It did."

00:13:06.240 --> 00:13:09.760
"In what way?"

00:13:09.760 --> 00:13:14.680
"I just felt like I
could do something.

00:13:16.080 --> 00:13:20.600
I had worth again,
so to speak.

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:24.600
Something that was important
to me was given back."

00:13:24.600 --> 00:13:27.040
Here’s what the head
of Harvard’s Brain Center

00:13:27.040 --> 00:13:29.120
at Mass General had to say:

00:13:29.120 --> 00:13:32.240
Big Pharma has invested
billions in the effort

00:13:32.240 --> 00:13:34.720
to ﬁnd medications
to treat the disease,

00:13:34.720 --> 00:13:39.120
but only two Alzheimer’s drugs have
been approved in the last 20 years—

00:13:39.120 --> 00:13:42.120
one of which was already
just pulled off the market,

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:45.360
and the other is minimally effective,
extremely expensive,

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:47.960
and often has
pesky side effects

00:13:47.960 --> 00:13:52.120
such as brain swelling
or bleeding into the brain.

00:13:52.120 --> 00:13:56.600
In contrast, the intensive lifestyle
changes implemented in this study

00:13:56.600 --> 00:13:59.040
have been shown here to
improve cognition and function,

00:13:59.800 --> 00:14:03.120
at the cost of, like,
buying broccoli,

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:08.366
and the only side
effects are positive ones.

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:11.320
We would love it if you could
share with us your stories

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:14.720
about reinventing your health
through evidence-based nutrition.

00:14:14.720 --> 00:14:17.640
Go to nutritionfacts.org/testimonials.

00:14:17.640 --> 00:14:22.080
We may share it on our social
media to help inspire others.

00:14:22.080 --> 00:14:26.840
If you want to see any graphs, charts,
graphics, images, or studies mentioned here,

00:14:26.840 --> 00:14:30.200
please go to the Nutrition Facts
Podcast landing page.

00:14:30.200 --> 00:14:32.240
There you’ll find all the
detailed information you need –

00:14:32.240 --> 00:14:36.680
plus, links to all of the sources
we cite for each of these topics.

00:14:36.680 --> 00:14:41.480
My latest book, “How Not to Age” has
been out for about half a year now.

00:14:41.480 --> 00:14:44.640
Check it out from your
local public library.

00:14:44.640 --> 00:14:47.080
And of course, all the proceeds
I receive of the sales

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:49.960
of all my books goes
directly to charity.

00:14:49.960 --> 00:14:53.400
NutritionFacts.org is a nonprofit,
science-based public service,

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:55.520
where you can sign up
for free daily updates

00:14:55.520 --> 00:14:59.040
on the latest in nutrition research
via bite-sized videos and articles.

00:14:59.840 --> 00:15:01.520
Everything on the
website is free.

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:02.400
There’s no ads,

00:15:02.400 --> 00:15:04.060
no corporate sponsorships,
no kickbacks.

00:15:04.060 --> 00:15:07.000
It’s strictly non-commercial.
I’m not selling anything.

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:10.160
I just put it up as a public service,
as a labor of love,

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:12.120
as a tribute to
my grandmother,

00:15:12.120 --> 00:15:16.600
